    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/420766100/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """late for the singing. Placci came to dinner, full of interest in the psychology of music. He has had two suggestive ideas already, both suggested by analogies in painting — namely that harmony is like modelling, and that the conventional endings of music (often a page or two long) are like the framing in of pictures. I decided to take music lessons from Buonamici, and went to bed quite excited with the idea. 
* Friday Nov. 6. 1896. Fiesole.
Still rain and rain and rain! A quiet day, with no people, but books and work.
Saturday Nov. 7. 1896.
We went down to Placci’s at 11 to hear him play with Buonamici - who, however, did not turn up. So we talked about music and he played from “The Magic Flute”. I asked why one wants a “frame” in time. The field of vision, being limited, naturally suggests a frame, but why time, or breath? """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 16 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1896-98> .

